    with some divergence in early April. Diesel grades (both standard and premium) consistently trade at a 
    premium to their petrol counterparts.
  </p>

  <div class="chart">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,""" + charts['trend'] + """" alt="Weekly mean price trend">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 7. Weekly mean price trend by fuel type from February to April 2026. All series show an upward drift consistent with rising crude oil and wholesale refined product costs during this period.</div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The gap between B7_STANDARD and E10 widened slightly over the period, from approximately 25ppl in 
    mid-February to nearly 30ppl by early April. This widening diesel premium has been a recurring 
    feature of UK fuel markets and may reflect both supply-side factors (refining margins) and 
    demand-side dynamics (commercial diesel demand recovery).
  </p>
</section>

<section id="key-findings">
  <h2>7. Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>

  <ol class="findings">
    <li>
      <strong>Diesel commands a persistent and widening premium over petrol.</strong> 
      B7_STANDARD averages 28.0ppl more than E10, and the gap has grown over the observation period. 
      <em>Policy implication:</em> Monitor diesel pricing closely for signs of market power or supply 
      constraints, particularly given diesel's importance to commercial transport and logistics.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>E10 shows the most competitive market structure.</strong> 
      With an IQR of just 12.2ppl (8.0% of median) and the smallest coefficient of variation, E10 prices 
      are tightly clustered. This suggests strong consumer price sensitivity and effective competition 
      for the UK's default petrol grade. <em>Policy implication:</em> The E10 market can be treated as 
      a benchmark for competitive pricing behaviour.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Premium fuels exhibit the widest price spreads and weakest convergence.</strong> 
      B7_PREMIUM has the largest IQR (23.0ppl) and the highest mean price (194.6ppl). Consumers of 
      premium grades face greater price dispersion and less transparent competition. 
      <em>Policy implication:</em> Consider requiring clearer price differentiation disclosures at 
      the pump, as premium fuel buyers may be less price-elastic and more vulnerable to excessive markups.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reporting freshness is generally excellent but with a concerning tail.</strong> 
      While 80.6% of records are captured within one hour, 3.5% are stale beyond 7 days. On the latest 
      record per station, 385 price points (1.9%) still exceed this threshold. 
      <em>Policy implication:</em> Introduce minimum freshness standards for mandated price reporting; 
      stations with consistently stale data should be flagged for compliance review.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>E5 reporting lags are systematically worse than other fuels.</strong> 
      Only 75.0% of E5 records are captured within one hour, versus ~82% for other mainstream fuels. 
      <em>Policy implication:</em> Investigate whether E5 data feeds use different technical infrastructure 
      or lower reporting priority, and target remediation efforts accordingly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Station coverage gaps limit consumer choice for premium grades.</strong> 
      B7_PREMIUM is available at only 44.0% of forecourts and E5 at 60.4%. Consumers in rural areas 
